  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 200,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1600,
  "polymer_wt": 15.5,
  "additive_wt": 0
}
```

**Rationale:** This experiment sits in the productive low-bath-temperature, moderate-wait-time, lower-polymer-weight region that has yielded the highest pore fractions in the dataset (0.65–0.85 for 14–15 wt% at 8–9°C). Increasing polymer_wt from 15.0 to 15.5 wt% is expected to modestly increase elastic modulus (higher polymer concentration → denser matrix) while the cold bath (8°C) and moderate evaporation time (200s) should preserve high pore fraction by promoting rapid phase separation. The pullcast speed of 6 mm/s and mixing_temp of 40°C are consistent with the productive cluster. This point is not yet sampled (nearest neighbors are 15wt%/8°C/250s and 15.5wt%/5°C/0s), providing genuine new information about the 15.5wt%/8°C/200s region. If successful, it could push the Pareto front by combining modulus >220 bar with pore fraction >0.70.

