### Validation Status

The **15-0add-9deg-N2-1600s** condition (polymer_wt=15, bath_temp=9, mixing_temp=40, pullcast_speed=6, N2=true, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600) has 7 runs. The best clean run (run1) shows 246.6/0.77 with high CV on modulus (SD=146 bar, ~59% CV — very high). Run2 shows 240.3/0.66 with wrinkle. Run3 shows 199/0.68 with wrinkle. Run4 shows 221/0.75 with top gap. Run5 shows 276.8/0.85 with top gap. Run6 shows 202/0.58 with bottom gap. Run7 shows 314.8/0.51 with clean membrane.

The pore fraction is highly variable (0.51–0.85) and modulus is also variable. The condition has not been reproducibly confirmed as a Pareto leader — the high variability and mixed quality prevent treating it as confirmed.

**14p5-10deg-N2-1500s** has 4 clean/near-clean runs with consistent pore fraction ~0.65–0.69 and modulus ~209–270 bar. This is more reproducible but lower pore fraction.

### What to explore next

The data suggests a promising region around **lower polymer concentration (14–15 wt%), cold bath (8–10°C), moderate wait time (200–300s), N2, moderate speed**. The 15 wt% / 9°C bath / 250s wait condition shows high pore fraction but high variability. 

A nearby unexplored point that could clarify the tradeoff and potentially improve both objectives: **polymer_wt=15, bath_temp=8°C** (slightly colder than 9°C to potentially boost modulus while maintaining high pore fraction), **mixing_temp=40°C, pullcast_speed=7 mm/s** (slightly faster than 6 to improve film uniformity and reduce wrinkles), **coupon_to_bath_wait_time=200s** (slightly shorter than 250s), **nips_bath_wait_time=1600s**. This is distinct from all existing points (no exact match at polymer_wt=15, bath_temp=8, speed=7, wait=200, nips=1600) and explores whether the high pore fraction of the 15/9°C region persists at 8°C while potentially improving modulus and membrane quality.

This is a genuine new exploration point in an under-sampled but promising region, not a near-duplicate of any existing condition.

