**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- **run5 (15wt, 9°C, 250s)**: 276.8 / 0.85 — highest pore fraction observed, but has coverage gap and very high CV (SD=50 bar, CV=0.08); single run
- **14p5-10deg-run2**: 209.1 / 0.69 — good quality, clean membrane
- **14-0add-8deg-run2**: 189.4 / 0.71 — good quality

The **15wt, 9°C bath, 250s** condition (runs 1–8) shows the most promising pore fraction values (0.51–0.85, mean ~0.68 across 8 runs) with modulus ranging 198–315 bar. Run 5 stands out with PF=0.85 but has a coverage gap. The condition overall shows high variability in pore fraction (SD across runs is large), suggesting the process is sensitive.

### Validation Status

The **15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s** condition has 8 runs with confirmed Polysulfone/PolarClean chemistry. The pore fraction values are: 0.77, 0.66, 0.68, 0.75, 0.85, 0.58, 0.51, 0.68. The modulus values are: 247, 240, 199, 221, 277, 202, 315, 211. This is a highly variable condition — the mean pore fraction is ~0.69 and mean modulus ~239 bar, which is genuinely strong on both objectives. However, run 5 (PF=0.85) appears to be an outlier.

The **14p5-10deg-200s-N2-1500s** condition has 4 runs with clean membranes and consistent pore fractions (0.67, 0.69, 0.66, 0.65) and modulus (222, 209, 255, 269). Mean ~0.67 PF, ~239 modulus — very reproducible and clean.

### Decision

The 15wt/9°C/250s condition has been run 8 times and shows high variability. The 14.5wt/10°C/200s condition has been run 4 times with good reproducibility and sits on the Pareto front with ~0.67 PF and ~239 bar modulus.

**What's underexplored:** The region of moderate polymer weight (14–15 wt%) with cold bath (8–10°C) and moderate wait times has been productive. A slightly different point — **14.5 wt%, bath 9°C, wait ~200s, speed ~6 mm/s** — would probe between the two validated clusters (14.5/10°C and 15/9°C) and could clarify whether the high pore fractions seen at 15wt/9°C are achievable with better reproducibility at a slightly lower polymer concentration and intermediate speed. This is a genuinely new point (not a near-duplicate of any existing condition) that could improve or clarify the Pareto front.

Expected benefit: Lower polymer concentration (14.5 wt%) tends to give higher pore fraction; cold bath (9°C) promotes rapid phase separation; moderate wait time (200s) and slow speed (6 mm/s) have shown clean membranes. This combination could yield PF ≥ 0.68 with modulus ≥ 200 bar in a clean membrane.

